From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 18:31:18 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-gpu: allocate host-visible blobs for in-kernel
 consumers

A guest driver that fills a buffer its client then renders from cannot use
guest pages: the renderer imports host memory and has no way to name them, so
the import is refused at PRIME_FD_TO_HANDLE and the client falls back to a
copy. The memory has to be a host-visible Virtio-GPU blob, which the host owns
and any renderer context can attach.

Nothing in the kernel could allocate one. The blob path belonged to a DRM
client's file descriptor: a context comes from a DRM file, and so does the
handle the blob is returned as. This adds a small in-kernel interface that
owns one renderer context for the kernel and hands out mappable blobs from it,
with mmap and dma-buf export, so another driver's buffer is the same object a
renderer can import.

The context is created lazily on the first allocation and destroyed with the
device. The blobs ask for VIRTGPU_BLOB_MEM_HOST3D with blob_id 0 and exactly
VIRTGPU_BLOB_FLAG_USE_MAPPABLE, which is what makes the renderer serve the
allocation out of its own shared memory rather than look for a host object,
and every size is rounded to the aperture's 16 KiB mapping granule so a buffer
is mappable whatever the guest page size.

The export goes through Virtio-GPU's own exporter rather than the generic DRM
one: an importer resolves a descriptor back to this object only when the
descriptor carries Virtio-GPU's dma-buf ops, and only that exporter names the
blob's shared-memory region to another virtio device.

The allocation sends the commands it queues (the context's creation the first
time, the blob's creation and its mapping) with virtio_gpu_notify(). A DRM
client's ioctl notifies after it queues, this caller has no ioctl, and its
client waits for the host's answer first, so without the call nothing sent the
queue until some other user of the device did: a stateful decoder's
VIDIOC_REQBUFS waited 53 to 60 seconds on a guest with nothing else to draw.

Separate from naming a blob's shared-memory region: that describes a blob a
DRM client already owns, while this one creates them for a driver that has no
DRM client at all.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
